¶
'''waftage''' (wȧf′tāj), ''n.'' [< ''waft'' + ''-age''.] The act of wafting, or the state of being wafted; conveyance or transportation through or over a buoyant medium, as air or water; especially, passage by water.
{{smaller block|A ship you sent me to, to hire ''waftage''.
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iv. 1. 95.}}
Not leaving him so much as a poor halfpenny to pay for his ''waftage''. {{right|''Randolph'', Jealous Lovers, iv. 4.}}}}

'''wafter''' (wȧf′tėr), ''n.'' [< ''waft'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who or that which wafts.
{{smaller block|<poem>Charon, oh, Charon,
Thou ''wafter'' of the souls to bliss or bane!</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Mad Lover, iv. 1.}}}}
'''2†.''' A boat for passage or transport.
{{smaller block|There went before the lord-mayor’s barge a foyste for a ''wafter'' full of ordinance.
{{right|Quoted in ''Strutt’s'' Sports and Pastimes, p. 479.}}}}
'''3†.''' The master of a passage-boat or transport.
{{smaller block|The . . . great master . . . sent vessels called brigantines, for to cause the ''wafters'' of the sea to come into Rhodes for the keeping and fortifying of the towne, the which at the first sending came and presented their persons and ships. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 75.}}}}
'''4.''' A sword having the flat part placed in the usual direction of the edge, blunted for exercises. ''Meyrick.'' (''Halliwell.'')

'''wafture''' (wȧf′t̮ūr), ''n.'' [< ''waft'' + ''-ure''.] The act of wafting or waving; a beckoning or gesture.
{{smaller block|<poem>But, with an angry ''wafture'' of your hand,
Gave sign for me to leave you.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., ii. 1. 246.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Where least expected, the Platonic seed seems blown by the continual ''wafture'' of the winds of destiny.
{{right|''Jour. Spec. Phil.'', XIX. 51.}}}}

'''wag<sup>1</sup>''' (wag), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''wagged'', ppr. ''wagging''. [< ME. ''waggen'', < OSw. ''wagga'', wag, fluctuate, rock (a cradle), Sw. ''vagga'', rock (a cradle) (cf. Icel. ''vagga'' = OSw. ''wagga'', Sw. ''vagga'', a cradle, = Dan. ''vugge'', a cradle, ''vugge'', rock a cradle); a secondary form (parallel with AS. ''wagian'', wag, > ME. ''wawen'' (see ''waw''<sup>2</sup>) = OHG. ''wagōn'', ''wecken'', cause to move, = Goth. ''wagjan'', ''gawagjan'', make wag, stir, shake) of AS. ''wegan'' = OHG. ''wegan'', move, = Goth. ''gawigan'', shake up, cause to move: see ''weigh''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cause to move up and down, backward and forward, or from side to side, alternately, as a small body jointed or attached to, or connected with, a larger one; cause to move one way or another, as on a pivot or joint, or on or from something by which the body moved is supported; cause to shake, oscillate, or vibrate slightly. {{smaller|From the quick, jerky, or abrupt motion indicated by the word, an idea of playful, sportive, mocking, scornful, or derisive motion is associated with it in certain phrases: as, to ''wag the head'' or ''the finger''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And thanne fondeth the Fende my fruit to destruye
With alle the wyles that he can, and ''waggeth'' the rote.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xvi. 41.}}
<poem>He found him selfe unwist so ill bestad
That lim he could not ''wag''. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., V. i. 22.}}</poem>
And they that passed by reviled him, ''wagging'' their heads. {{right|Mat. xxvii. 39.}}
<poem>Let ditch-bred wealth henceforth forget to ''wag''
Her base, though golden tail.</poem>
{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, ii. 12.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>Let me see the proudest
. . . but ''wag'' his finger at thee.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., v. 3. 131.}}}}
{{smaller block|He would plant himself straight before me, and stand ''wagging'' that bud of a tail. {{right|''Dr. J. Brown'', Rab, p. 12.}}}}
'''2†.''' To nudge.
{{smaller block|Ich wondrede what that was, and ''waggede'' Conscience; . . . Quath Conscience, . . . “this is Cristes messager.” {{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xxii. 204.}}}}
'''To wag one’s chin''' or '''jaw.''' {{smaller|See ''chin.''}}—'''To wag one’s tongue.''' {{smaller|See ''tongue.''}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To move backward and forward, up and down, or from side to side, alternately, as if connected with a larger body by a joint, pivot, or any flexible or loose attachment; oscillate; sway or swing; vibrate: an arrow is said to ''wag'' when it vibrates in the air.
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet saugh I nevere, by my fader kyn,
How that the hopur [hopper] ''wagges'' til and fra.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 119.}}}}
{{smaller block|Old men are the truest lovers; young men are inconstant, and ''wag'' with every wind. {{right|''Shirley'', Love Tricks, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The dreary black sea-weed lolls and ''wags.''</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Appledore, i.}}}}
'''2.''' To be in motion or action; make progress; continue a course or career; stir. [Now colloq.]
{{smaller block|“Thus we may see,” quoth he, “how the world ''wags.''” {{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 7. 23.}}}}
{{smaller block|They made a pretty good shift to ''wag'' along. {{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, ii.}}}}
'''3.''' To move on or away; be off; depart; pack off; be gone. [Now colloq.]

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|It is said by maner of a prouerbiall speach that he who findes himselfe well should not ''wagge''.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 194.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>At length the busy time begins.
“Come, neighbours, we must ''wag''.”</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Yearly Distress.}}}}

'''wag'''<sup>1</sup> (wag), ''n.'' [< ''wag''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] The act of wagging; a shake; an oscillation.
{{smaller block|He . . . introduced himself with a ''wag'' of his tail, intimating a general willingness to be happy.
{{right|''Dr. J. Brown'', Spare Hours, 1st ser., p. 87.}}}}

'''wag'''<sup>2</sup> (wag), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''wagge''; perhaps short for ''waghalter'', formerly used humorously for ‘a rogue’ (cf. ‘a mad ''wag''’ with ‘a mad ''waghalter''’), < ''wag''<sup>1</sup>, with ref. to moving the head playfully or derisively: see ''wag''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who is given to joking or jesting; a witty or humorous person; one full of sport and humor; a droll fellow. {{smaller|The word seems formerly to have been applied to a person who indulged in coarse, low, or broad humor, or buffoonery, as a practical joker.}}
{{smaller block|''Sir Fran.'' A prodigious civil gentleman, uncle; and yet as bold as Alexander upon occasion.<br>''Unc. Rich.'' Upon a lady’s occasion.<br>''Sir Fran.'' Ha, ha, you are a ''wag'', uncle.
{{right|''Vanbrugh'', Journey to London, iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|A ''wag'' is the last order even of pretenders to wit and good humour. He has generally his mind prepared to receive some occasion of merriment, but is of himself too empty to draw out any of his own set of thoughts; and therefore laughs at the next thing he meets, not because it is ridiculous, but because he is under a necessity of laughing.
{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 184.}}}}
